1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:08,952
Tuesday morning, Bekzod sits down to connect Malika's website to the bank, and Malika sends him a message: just use my password, it's easier.

2
00:00:09,002 --> 00:00:11,232
It is easier, for about a month.

3
00:00:11,282 --> 00:00:22,344
In Lesson 18, two admins switched Malika's company on, and in Lesson 1, the shop's server asked ecom to create each payment, and this lesson gives that server a login of its own.

4
00:00:22,394 --> 00:00:26,256
Her password in a server's settings ends up in a shared code store.

5
00:00:26,306 --> 00:00:30,072
When Bekzod moves on, Malika's own login has to change.

6
00:00:30,122 --> 00:00:35,328
And every request the server ever makes looks like Malika clicking buttons.

7
00:00:35,378 --> 00:00:39,432
I will say it plainly: a server should never log in as a person.

8
00:00:39,482 --> 00:00:43,728
So Bekzod opens API clients in the merchant portal.

9
00:00:43,778 --> 00:00:48,624
Yesterday, before the company was switched on, this page had no button at all.

10
00:00:48,674 --> 00:00:56,304
It said API clients open when you are live", and explained that two bank admins must activate the account first.

11
00:00:56,354 --> 00:01:01,008
Only an active merchant can create or rotate an API client.

12
00:01:01,058 --> 00:01:06,072
Today the page explains that the merchant's servers use these to get access tokens.

13
00:01:06,122 --> 00:01:08,112
Bekzod clicks New client.

14
00:01:08,162 --> 00:01:12,360
An API client is a login for a machine, not for a person.

15
00:01:12,410 --> 00:01:15,864
A window opens: "Your new API client".

16
00:01:15,914 --> 00:01:23,016
It shows the client id, like a username, and the client secret, like a password that only the machine knows.

17
00:01:23,066 --> 00:01:29,904
Above them is a warning: this is the only time you will see this secret, so store it in your server's secret manager now.

18
00:01:29,954 --> 00:01:34,608
Bekzod copies both, and ticks "I have stored the secret safely".

19
00:01:34,658 --> 00:01:36,480
The window closes.

20
00:01:36,530 --> 00:01:40,968
The list shows the new client with its id and the date it was created.

21
00:01:41,018 --> 00:01:44,736
The secret is nowhere on the page, and it never will be again.

22
00:01:44,786 --> 00:01:48,120
ecom keeps the client's id, but never the secret.

23
00:01:48,170 --> 00:01:53,256
So nobody at the bank, and nobody who breaks into ecom's database, can read it back.

24
00:01:53,306 --> 00:01:57,768
Now the website's server sends its id and secret to the login server.

25
00:01:57,818 --> 00:02:01,728
It gets back a short-lived token: a pass that expires soon.

26
00:02:01,778 --> 00:02:04,584
Only that token reaches the payments service.

27
00:02:04,634 --> 00:02:08,520
And only a machine login with the merchant's role can reach it at all.

28
00:02:08,570 --> 00:02:11,472
People's logins cannot, not even Malika's.

29
00:02:11,522 --> 00:02:16,296
On Thursday, Bekzod pastes the secret into a group chat by mistake.

30
00:02:16,346 --> 00:02:18,168
He does not panic.

31
00:02:18,218 --> 00:02:20,040
He clicks Rotate secret.

32
00:02:20,090 --> 00:02:26,400
The portal asks first: the current secret stops working immediately, so update your servers right after.

33
00:02:26,450 --> 00:02:28,272
He confirms.

34
00:02:28,322 --> 00:02:31,920
A new secret appears, once, with the same warning.

35
00:02:31,970 --> 00:02:37,704
That is rotation: a new secret is issued, and the old one stops working at once.

36
00:02:37,754 --> 00:02:42,168
Anyone who tries the old secret from the chat is refused by the login server.

37
00:02:42,218 --> 00:02:46,896
Malika's own login is never touched, and she only hears about it at lunch.

38
00:02:46,946 --> 00:02:50,592
A separate key for each server keeps the owner's login private.

39
00:02:50,642 --> 00:02:56,016
It lets the merchant cut off one leaked key in one click, without locking anyone out.

40
00:02:56,066 --> 00:03:01,008
And because ecom never keeps the secret, the bank cannot leak it either.

41
00:03:01,058 --> 00:03:03,432
Next: the people on Malika's team.
